\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Polarization and positivity of the quadratic weight}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{Q} be a \vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004}
on a finite-dimensional real inner-product space #{(E,h)}, with
associated #{b_Q,q_Q}, \vocabk{Euclidean quadratic action}{fdip-0004}
#{S_Q} and \vocabk{Boltzmann weight}{fdip-0004} #{w_Q}.
Then #{b_Q} is symmetric bilinear, and for #{x,y\in E}, #{c\in\mathbb R},
##{\begin{aligned}
q_Q(x+y)-q_Q(x)-q_Q(y)&=2b_Q(x,y),\\
q_Q(cx)&=c^2q_Q(x),\\
q_Q&=2S_Q,\\
S_Q(x+y)-S_Q(x)-S_Q(y)&=b_Q(x,y).
\end{aligned}}
The action satisfies #{S_Q(0)=0} and #{S_Q(x)>0} for #{x\ne0}.
Both #{S_Q} and #{w_Q} are continuous and Borel measurable, and
##{0<w_Q(x)\leq1,\qquad w_Q(x)=1\ \Longleftrightarrow\ x=0.}}
\proof{
\p{Linearity of #{Q} and the pairing gives bilinearity of #{b_Q}.
Self-adjointness and symmetry of #{h} give
##{b_Q(y,x)=h(y,Qx)=h(Qy,x)=h(x,Qy)=b_Q(x,y).}
Expanding #{b_Q(x+y,x+y)} proves the polarization identity, and
bilinearity gives #{q_Q(cx)=c^2q_Q(x)}. Thus #{q_Q} has the
quadratic-form properties with companion #{2b_Q}.
The definition #{S_Q=q_Q/2} gives #{q_Q=2S_Q}; dividing the
polarization identity by two gives the action identity.
Strict positivity of #{Q} gives the asserted values and signs of #{S_Q}.}
\p{For continuity, choose an orthonormal basis #{u_i} in this proof
and write #{x=\sum_i y_i u_i}. The finite coordinate formula is
##{S_Q(x)=\frac12\sum_{i,j}h(u_i,Qu_j)y_i y_j.}
It is a polynomial in continuous coordinates, hence continuous and
Borel measurable, using the coordinate criterion in \ref{fdip-002G}. Its exponential is continuous and Borel
as well. Since #{S_Q\geq0}, the exponential is strictly positive and
at most one; equality holds exactly where #{S_Q=0}, hence at #{x=0}.
The empty coordinate family gives the same conclusions in dimension zero.}
}
\p{The bound #{w_Q\leq1} alone does not prove integrability on a
space of infinite reference volume. The Gaussian normalization theorem \ref{fdip-002T} supplies that
separate argument.}
\p{These identities expand the \vocabk{quadratic action}{fdip-0004}, with
the half-action and exponential convention of
\citet{Sections 3-4, Equations (3.1) and (4.5), pp. 2-3}{wingate2025lattice}.
Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.BilinMap.toQuadraticMap} supplies the
diagonal quadratic-map construction for a bilinear map. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.BilinMap.polarBilin_toQuadraticMap_of_flip}
gives the factor #{2} for a symmetric bilinear form. Continuity and
the weight bounds use the real precision hypotheses.}
